window.docContents[162].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Small-rigorous-improvement":{"contents":"* Question* Context change* Attribute change* Risk shown* PhysLib stage\n* The pair now has bodies, a coupling spring, and multiple records. What must hold, for which systems or objects?* Give the nominal pair IDs P and Q a Sorted instance into pairS, and designate bodies and the coupling spring.* Pair mass, normal-mode frequency, coupling-spring extension, requirement scope, referent, and gate.* An unscoped claim cannot say whether it applies to every pair or to every state of P; a bare real and a non-sort are rejected as scopes.* Stage 4 requirements and audits.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L6 — What must hold","header":"5.7.5. Small rigorous improvement","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Small-rigorous-improvement"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Quantities___-numbers___-and-the-count":{"contents":"* This work* Dybkær (2009)* ISO 80000-1:2022* VIM 3 (JCGM 200:2012)* VIM 4 2CD* Quantity* §13.3.3* 4.1 quantity* 1.1 quantity* 1.1 quantity* Carrier* §13.5 (extensive aggregation)* —* —* —* LawfulCarrier* no section given* —* —* —* instCarrierNat* no section given* 5 (dimension 1; counts not named)* 1.8 Note 4 (quantity of dimension one)* 1.10 quantity with unit one; 1.6 Note 4* Part9.numberOfEntities* no section given* 5 (dimension 1; counts not named)* 1.8 Note 4* 1.6 Note 4; 1.2 Note 3* Quantity.instLE* §12.16* 4.1 (comparable by order)* 1.26 ordinal quantity* 1.33 Note 2* Quantity.instDecidableLE* no section given* —* —* —* DifferenceKind* §12.19* —* 1.21 quantity calculus* 1.29 quantity calculus; 1.4 interval quantity\n\n* Agreement. The count is a quantity. VIM 3 1.8 Note 4 states that numbers of entities\nare quantities of dimension one, and VIM 4 2CD adds that number of entities can be\nregarded as a base quantity. This work carries a count in Nat (instCarrierNat), at\na ratio-scale kind, and its ISO 80000-9 catalogue entry numberOfEntities has\ndimension one. A count is therefore a Quantity k Nat of a declared kind and never a\nbare number: two counts of different kinds cannot be added or compared.* ISO 80000-1:2022 does not name the count. Clause 5 treats quantities whose\ndimensional exponents are all zero, notes that there is no agreed name for them, and\nsays that such quantities are dimensionally simply numbers. It does not mention\nnumbers of entities. Here a dimension-one quantity keeps its kind, so it is never\nmerely a number.* Quantity k R is narrower than quantity. In ISO 80000-1 and the VIM a quantity is\na property, and the word covers both the general and the individual quantity. A\nQuantity k R is a magnitude of one fixed kind k, held in a number type R. It has\nno bearer and no unit. The general quantity is the kind k; the individual quantity is\nIndividualQuantity o k R; and the number-and-unit form is produced by\nMetrologicalUnit.measure.* Quantity k R is not restricted by scale. The type can be formed at any kind,\nnominal included. What the scale restricts is the operations: addition and subtraction\nrequire a DifferenceKind, and products, quotients and reciprocals require the kind\nedges below. This matches quantity calculus in VIM 3 1.21, which excludes ordinal\nquantities, and in VIM 4 2CD 1.29, which applies to ratio and interval quantities.* The order is per kind. Quantity.instLE compares two quantities of the same kind\nonly; a comparison across kinds does not type-check. Quantity.instDecidableLE makes\nit decidable whenever the carrier's order is. The instance exists at every kind whose\ncarrier is ordered; whether the order is meaningful for the kind is the separate\ncondition AllowsOrder, which bounds require.* No counterpart: the number type. ISO 80000-1 and the VIM take \"number\" as given.\nCarrier and LawfulCarrier state what a number type must provide (a zero and an\naddition, then the laws of a commutative monoid). Nat and Int are lawful carriers.\nThe sources have no term for this, and it is where a vocabulary of numbers attaches to\na vocabulary of quantities.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Standards Reference — PKC beside ISO 80000-1 and the VIM","header":"34.5. Quantities, numbers, and the count","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Quantities___-numbers___-and-the-count"}});